(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["cam-nutrition-exam-1-nutrition-aging-quiz"]={"title":"Nutrition & Aging Quiz (Set 1) — CAM/Nutrition Exam 1","category":"CAM / Nutrition","questions":[{"q":"According to the lecture, although adults over 65 years make up less than 15% of the United States population, what share of all prescription medications, hospitalizations, and the federal health budget do they account for?","choices":["About 80%","About 35%","About 15%","About 55%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the lecture states people over 65 account for about 35% of all prescription medications, hospitalizations, and the federal health budget. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"The lecture identifies which group as the fastest-growing segment of the population, projected to reach 19 million by 2050?","choices":["Adolescents completing physical growth","Adults aged 30 to 40 years","People aged 85 years and older","People aged 65 to 74 years"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: adults 85 and older are described as the fastest-growing segment, projected to reach 19 million by 2050. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"The lecture states that once adolescence and physical growth are complete, the body's use of nutrients shifts primarily toward which purpose?","choices":["Rapid growth and tissue expansion","Storage of excess fat for later growth","Building peak bone mass","Maintenance rather than growth"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: in adulthood nutrients switch to maintenance instead of growth. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"How does the lecture define the concept of an organ's \"reserve capacity\" in aging?","choices":["The rate at which new cells replace dying cells","The ability to maintain normal function despite a decreased number or activity of cells","The extra calories stored as fat for use during illness","The maximum number of years a human can live"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: reserve capacity lets organs keep normal function even with fewer or less active cells, until that reserve is exhausted. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"According to the lecture, after roughly what age does the rate of cell breakdown slowly begin to exceed the rate of cell renewal?","choices":["Age 50 to 60 years","Age 65 to 70 years","Age 30 to 40 years","Age 18 to 20 years"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: after age 30 to 40, the rate of cell breakdown slowly exceeds the rate of cell renewal. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"The lecture describes a \"thrifty phenotype.\" Which set of traits best matches it?","choices":["Requires more calories and resists storing any body fat","Maintains a constant weight regardless of intake","Requires fewer calories for metabolism and stores body fat easily","Burns energy quickly when overeating with little drop during fasting"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the thrifty phenotype needs fewer calories and stores fat easily, historically aiding survival through famine. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A person whose body burns energy quickly when overeating and shows little energy drop while fasting, and who tends to gain less weight, fits which description from the lecture?","choices":["Sedentary phenotype","Spendthrift phenotype","Thrifty phenotype","Sarcopenic phenotype"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the spendthrift phenotype burns energy quickly during overeating and is associated with less weight gain. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"How does the lecture distinguish \"life span\" from \"life expectancy\"?","choices":["Life span applies to women and life expectancy applies to men","Life span is the maximum years a human can live; life expectancy is the average years a person born in a given year and place is expected to live","Life span is the average years lived; life expectancy is the biological maximum","Both terms mean the same number of years for any individual"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: life span is the biological maximum, while life expectancy is the statistical average for a birth cohort. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"The lecture attributes the maximum human life span primarily to which combination of influences?","choices":["Genes alone, independent of behavior","Prescription medication use","Body surface area and heat loss","Genes, lifestyle, and environment"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: the longest human life spans are described as dependent on genes, lifestyle, and environment. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What do the five \"Blue Zones\" identified by Dan Buettner and National Geographic have in common?","choices":["The highest numbers of centenarians with low rates of heart disease, diabetes, obesity, and cancer","The highest total populations in the world","The highest average daily calorie intakes","The lowest average life expectancies"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: Blue Zones are regions with the most centenarians and low rates of those chronic diseases. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"According to the lecture, the eating pattern shared across the Blue Zones most closely resembles which diet?","choices":["A high-protein carnivore diet","A very-low-carbohydrate ketogenic diet","The Mediterranean diet","The Standard American Diet"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: Blue Zones follow a Mediterranean-style pattern with the lowest rates of chronic disease. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"In the Blue Zones dietary pattern, what is described as the main dietary fat?","choices":["Coconut oil","Lard","Olive oil","Butter"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: olive oil is identified as the main dietary fat in the Blue Zones pattern. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"The lecture characterizes the Standard American Diet as high in which combination?","choices":["Unsaturated oils and legumes","Animal fat, simple carbohydrates, and sodium","Fiber, potassium, and plant-based foods","Complex carbohydrates and lean protein"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the Standard American Diet is described as high in animal fat, simple carbohydrates, and sodium. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"The \"DETERMINE\" checklist taught in the lecture is used for what purpose?","choices":["To identify warning signs of poor nutrition in older adults","To calculate an older adult's basal metabolic rate","To diagnose specific vitamin deficiencies from blood tests","To rank foods by their glycemic index"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: DETERMINE is an acronym used to identify warning signs of poor nutritional health. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"In the \"DETERMINE\" acronym, the letter \"M\" stands for which warning sign of poor nutrition?","choices":["Multiple medications","Missing teeth","Malnutrition","Muscle weakness"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: M represents multiple medications, one of the warning signs in the DETERMINE checklist. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"How does the lecture define basal metabolic rate (BMR)?","choices":["The calories stored as adipose tissue","The number of calories in a typical older adult's meal","The total calories the body needs to perform basic life-sustaining functions","The calories burned only during physical exercise"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: basal metabolic rate is the total number of calories needed for basic life-sustaining functions. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"According to the lecture, approximately how much does basal metabolic rate (BMR) decline per decade in adulthood?","choices":["It does not decline with age","About 2% per decade","About 10% per decade","About 25% per decade"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the lecture states basal metabolic rate declines about 2% per decade. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"The lecture emphasizes \"no empty calories\" for older adults mainly because a lower basal metabolic rate means they should prioritize which kind of foods?","choices":["Calorie-dense processed snacks","Foods highest in saturated fat","Foods highest in added sugar","Nutrient-dense foods"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: with fewer calories needed, older adults should choose nutrient-dense foods rather than empty calories. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What protein intake does the lecture recommend for older adults to help preserve muscle and bone mass?","choices":["1.0 to 1.2 grams per kilogram per day","0.4 to 0.6 grams per kilogram per day","2.5 to 3.0 grams per kilogram per day","No specific amount is recommended"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: the recommended intake is 1.0 to 1.2 grams of protein per kilogram per day. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"The lecture notes that excessive protein intake in older adults may pose what risk?","choices":["Reduced satiety and overeating","Increased glycemic index of meals","Accelerated decline in kidney function","Rapid loss of bone mass"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: excessive protein may accelerate decline in kidney function, though it is rare because protein is satiating. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"According to the lecture, why does the fiber in whole grains help with glucose control in older adults?","choices":["It converts complex carbohydrates into simple sugars","It eliminates the need for insulin entirely","It raises the glycemic index of a meal","It helps stabilize blood glucose, unlike simple carbohydrates that raise it quickly"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: whole-grain fiber stabilizes glucose, while simple carbohydrates cause hyperglycemia and eventual glycosylation. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What daily fiber intake does the lecture recommend for adults?","choices":["25 to 35 grams per day","5 to 10 grams per day","50 to 60 grams per day","100 grams per day"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: the recommended fiber intake is 25 to 35 grams per day. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"The lecture lists a fading sensitivity to the thirst sensation as one reason older adults may have low fluid intake. What daily fluid target does it suggest?","choices":["About six to eight 8-ounce glasses daily","About one 8-ounce glass daily","About sixteen 8-ounce glasses daily","Fluid intake does not need to be monitored"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: the lecture suggests aiming for six to eight 8-ounce glasses of water daily. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"The lecture notes vitamin B12 (cobalamin) is a special concern in older adults because its absorption declines with age for what reason?","choices":["The kidneys excrete it more rapidly","It is destroyed by weight-bearing exercise","It binds to dietary fiber and is lost","Low stomach acid secretion reduces its absorption"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: vitamin B12 absorption declines with age due to reduced stomach acid secretion. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"According to the lecture, a deficiency of which micronutrient increases the risk of osteoporosis and muscle weakness, with recommended intake rising to 800 international units daily at age 70 and older?","choices":["Vitamin D","Vitamin E","Zinc","Iron"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: vitamin D deficiency raises osteoporosis and muscle-weakness risk, with 800 international units recommended at age 70 and older. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"The lecture states zinc absorption often decreases in older adults. What is the main reason and consequence?","choices":["Rapid renal excretion causes muscle weakness","Decreased stomach acid lowers absorption, leading to a decline in immunity","Increased stomach acid destroys dietary zinc, causing anemia","Excess fiber intake blocks it, causing bone loss"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: reduced stomach acid decreases zinc absorption, and deficiency causes a decline in immunity. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"How does the lecture define sarcopenia?","choices":["Accumulation of excess adipose tissue only","An increase in total body water","Thinning of bone leading to fracture","Loss of lean body mass as muscle cells shrink or are lost"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: sarcopenia is the loss of lean body mass, with muscle cells shrinking or being lost. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"The lecture uses the term \"sarcopenic obesity\" to describe which combination?","choices":["Bone loss combined with dehydration","Advanced muscle loss accompanied by gains in fat mass","High muscle mass with very low body fat","Loss of both muscle and fat during illness"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: sarcopenic obesity is advanced muscle loss occurring together with gains in fat mass. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which set of measures does the lecture recommend to help preserve bone mass with aging?","choices":["Bed rest with a very-low-protein diet","Increased alcohol and cola intake","Smoking to suppress appetite","Weight-bearing exercise plus adequate vitamin D, calcium, and protein"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: weight-bearing exercise with adequate vitamin D, calcium, and protein helps preserve bone mass. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"The lecture notes a decline in hydrochloric acid, intrinsic factor, and digestive enzymes with age most directly lowers absorption of which vitamin?","choices":["Vitamin B12","Vitamin C","Vitamin K","Vitamin D"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: reduced hydrochloric acid and intrinsic factor lower absorption of vitamin B12. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 44"}]};
